INT. SCHOOL ROOM - DAY

Is this your first time using Beat? This file is a playground for you to mess around with. For more detailed instructions, see Documentation on Beat website.

In Beat, you don't need to press any special keys to write dialogue or a scene header. Just type "int. school" or "ext. park" on a new line and it will become a scene header.

Try it out yourself!


EXT. PARK - DAY

The same is true with dialogue. Just type in CHARACTER NAME (all caps) and then follow it with a dialogue block (or parenthetical) on the next line. It will become dialogue. 

Try it out!

CHARACTER
Hello. This is your first dialogue block.

CHARACTER 2
(gently nodding their head)
Well done.

You can also press Tab to force a character cue and not worry about writing in all caps.

After one empty line break the format will change back to action. Beat automatically adds two line breaks after certain elements, unless you tell it not to (Format → Automatic Paragraph Breaks).

You can also use transitions just by typing out something in all-caps and ending it with a colon, like this:

CUT TO:

Well, that was easy. You should be able to write a full screenplay by using those elements. 

Now, if you dare, let's get more technical!


/*

This is a note. It won't be printed or exported, don't worry. This way, you can add longer comments and omit scenes.

Oh, and check out the print preview, either by clicking on the eye icon, File → Toggle preview, or by pressing ⌘⌥P)

The title page info you see at the beginning of this file will be printed out as a very nice title page. You can add the title page stuff from Format → Add title page or use Format → Title Page Editor

*/


# This is a new section

= This is a synopsis line. Let's get a bit more technical.

INT. TUNNEL - NIGHT 

You can use sections and synopses (as above) to help structure your story. They won't be printed out, don't worry.

Take a look at the outline view (top-left icon or ⌘D) and see how the structure is laid out! Index Card View (found on top bar menu) is a useful tool for visualizing and laying out your story beats.


INT./EXT. BEACH HOUSE - NIGHT

[[Stylizing your script. If you use a note as the first paragraph, it will show up as the description of this scene in the index card view.]]

Text formatting can be done with the usual commands used in any software, (⌘B, ⌘I, etc.). Because **Beat** uses Fountain markup language, some strange characters will show up on the screen, but don't you worry. *They won't print out*.

Basically, text formatting works so that anything inside dual asterisks is **bolded** and single asterisks make it *italic*. You can also _underline_ stuff using underscores.

You can also force any element when it's not recognized automatically. There's a menu button at the bottom of the editor view with all sorts of formatting tools.  Try it now!

Text can be centered by surrounding it with > and <

>Centered text<

Transitions can be forced using single >

> Forced, surprisingly slow transition...


EXT. STREET - NIGHT [[BLUE]]

= Dual dialogue & other special elements

You can set the color for your scene just by typing it out as above, or by right-clicking a scene either in any of the other views (Outline, Index Cards, Timeline).

Double dialogue can be written by adding a ^ symbol after a character name.

CHARACTER
Hello, have you heard about Beat?

CHARACTER 2 ^
(interrupting)
Leave me alone.


INT. NIGHT CLUB - NIGHT [[RED]]

Here's some lyrics:

~”If you come to my house, friend
~bring me a lamp and a window I can look through
~at the crowd in the happy alley.”

That's about it. Edit this file and try out stuff. If you ever mess something up, you can usually reformat it by just removing something and adding it back.


EXT. UNIVERSITY CLASSROOM - MORNING

You can use revisions to keep track of your changes. Either toggle on *Revision Mode* from document settings, or select a range and click on the menu with asterisk (*) to mark a range as revised.

[[marker You can also add markers to indicate parts that need work or attention.]]

[[marker pink: This is a pink marker!]]

You can find more information about the Fountain markup language at https://www.fountain.io/

> FADE OUT






